	"descriptionMarkdown": "Playing SeaBlock, I was struggling with SO MANY INTERMEDIATE PRODUCTS OMG!!\n\nI thought about having Sushi Trains going round and round, but I thought that would be inefficient, and why not use 2.0 train interrupts?\n\nOh, yeah... because SeaBlock has not been released on 2.0 yet! Well, worry not because there is an unofficial (semi-official?) fork, and it works well for the most part: https://github.com/KompetenzAirbag/SeaBlock/tree/2.0-logic-changes\n\nSummary:\n- `Trains` have 4 interrupts:\n   - Refuel\n   - Go to Sink (requester)\n   - Go to Source (provider)\n   - Lost, back to Depot\n- `Sink` (requester) Station:\n   - Configurable item kind and stacks per kind.\n   - Triggers request when buffer is under configured limit.\n   - Request transmitted over Radar (configurable channel)\n   - Extract items from train until idle.\n- `Source` (provider) & `Refuel` Stations:\n   - Configurable item kind and stacks per kind.\n   - Opens when buffer is over configured limit.\n   - Insert items into train until idle.\n- `Depot` Station:\n   - Configurable item kind and stacks per kind (expected train contents!)\n   - Dispatch train to `Source` if the train has less than configured item kind. (Relies on inventory filters)\n   - Dispatch train to `Sink` if the Radar has a pending request \n   - Prioritizes `Source` over `Sink`\n\n**IMPORTANT**:\n- For this blueprint to work, your train wagons MUST rely on inventory filters so they can carry more than one payload.\n- The reason I added channels to the Radar is to support multiple rail networks. You can turn them off if you don't need that.\n- Trains \n\nBlueprint imported from: https://factoriobin.com/post/8jy7mx",
